\subsection{Launch Cadence and Pad Turnaround}

A campaign of {{ run.result.total_launches }}~launches
averages
{{ "%.2f"|format(run.result.total_launches / run.result.total_periods) }}~launches
per period, or roughly
{{ "%.2f"|format(run.result.total_launches / (run.result.total_periods * 7 / 30)) }}~per
calendar month. This cadence is consistent with a mixed-fleet
architecture in which multiple vehicles share the load, since
the pad-turnaround constraint (Section~II.G) precludes any
single vehicle from flying faster than once every
$\Delta t_{\mathrm{pad}}$~periods.

\subsection{Schedule and Crew Rotation}

At {{ run.result.total_periods }}~periods
({{ "%.1f"|format(run.result.total_periods * 7 / 30) }}~months),
the campaign length is governed by the interaction of
assembly-hour availability (Eqs.~\ref{eq:eva}--\ref{eq:crew_hours}),
DAG precedence, and proximity penalties. Gaps between crew
rotations appear as flat regions in the modules-completed step
curve (Fig.~\ref{fig:modules_1}); their aggregate duration is
a sensitive function of the crew rotation cadence and the
60\% duty-cycle assumption.

\subsection{Cost Structure}

The per-vehicle cost decomposition
(Fig.~\ref{fig:cost_1}) reveals which launchers dominate the
program budget in this scenario. Because the cost function
(Eq.~\ref{eq:cost}) is normalized, a heavy-lift vehicle with a
high unit cost but favorable \$/kg can displace many mid-sized
flights and still reduce the weighted objective.

\subsection{Safety}

The cumulative proximity risk of
{{ "%.4f"|format(run.result.cumulative_risk) }} is driven by the
$\binom{n}{2}$ pair term in Eq.~(\ref{eq:risk}); periods with
simultaneous crew arrival and cargo unloading are the primary
contributors, visible as crew/vehicle count peaks in
Fig.~\ref{fig:resources_1}.

