\subsection{Launches versus Duration}

The Pareto scatter in Fig.~\ref{fig:pareto} shows that reducing
total launches by flying fewer, larger vehicles tends to
lengthen the campaign: heavy-lift payloads arrive less
frequently, crew-rotation gaps widen, and partial assembly
(WIP) accumulates. Conversely, schedule-minimal solutions
saturate the pad-turnaround constraint and push launch count
toward its upper bound of $2|\mathcal{M}_{\mathrm{total}}|$.
The frontier is close to linear over the sampled weight grid,
suggesting a roughly constant marginal exchange rate between
the two objectives within this parameter regime.

\subsection{Cost Sensitivity}

Total program cost spans a
{{ "%.1f"|format(max_cost / min_cost) if min_cost > 0 else 0 }}$\times$
range across the ensemble. Because crew vehicles incur a fixed
per-flight premium and transfer stages amortize only when
reused, cost is most sensitive to crew rotation frequency and
to whether the selected cargo vehicles are L4-direct or
require tug assistance
(Eqs.~\ref{eq:rocket}--\ref{eq:tug}).

\subsection{Safety Trade-Offs}

Cumulative risk (Eq.~\ref{eq:risk}) scales with the square of
simultaneous vehicle presence. Scenarios that minimize
duration by compressing launches into overlapping windows
incur disproportionately higher risk; the congestion penalty
$\pi(n,p)$ already captures part of this effect in the cost
function, but the residual risk metric exposes the remainder
and could be promoted to an explicit fourth objective in
future work.

\subsection{Practical Recommendations}

For operational planning, the Pareto points in
Fig.~\ref{fig:pareto} that minimize launches at the expense of
a modest duration increase are attractive: the incremental
schedule cost is bounded while launch-stack complexity and
proximity risk decrease together. Schedule-minimal operating
points are justified only when external deadlines dominate the
decision calculus.

